 be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...A portion of south central Colorado, including the\nfollowing areas, the Eastern San Juan Mountains, and the Upper Rio\nGrande Valley.\n\n* WHEN...From late Saturday night through Monday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Heavy rainfall is expected to fall Sunday and Monday across the\narea, leading to elevated concerns for flooding in areas that\nhave recently received excessive moisture, or have recently\nexperienced flooding.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T12:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Upper Rio Grande Valley/Eastern San Juan Mountains Below 10000 Ft; Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.6c702f44556c2240320a73dcc302b9a6b4962366.002.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.6c702f44556c2240320a73dcc302b9a6b4962366.002.1"},{"event":"Winter Storm Watch","headline":"Winter Storm Watch issued October 30 at 11:17AM MDT until November 2 at 11:00AM MST by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Heavy snow possible. Total snow accumulations between 8 and\n14 inches possible. Winds could gust as high as 55 mph.\n\n* WHERE...Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Feet County.\n\n* WHEN...From Sunday afternoon through Monday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Travel could be very difficult, especially Sunday night\nand Monday morning. Strong winds could produce areas of blowing and\ndrifting snow.","ends":"2026-11-02T12:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.bc72b7787d05fc502cc55995c1a335250b63be22.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.bc72b7787d05fc502cc55995c1a335250b63be22.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued October 29 at 9:42PM MDT until November 2 at 11:00AM MST by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall is possible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of southwest Colorado, including the following\nareas, Animas River Basin, Four Corners/Upper Dolores River, San\nJuan River Basin and Southwest San Juan Mountains and southeast\nUtah, including the following areas, Canyonlands/Natural Bridges,\nLa Sal and Abajo Mountains and Southeast Utah.\n\n* WHEN...From Sunday morning through Monday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, recently burned areas, and other low-lying and\nflood-prone locations. Low-water crossings may be flooded. Storm\ndrains and ditches may become clogged with debris.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- 1 to 3 inches of rain is expected across portions of southeast\nUtah and southwest Colorado from Sunday morning through Monday\nmorning over soils that are still saturated from recent rainfall\nand flooding.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T12:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Southwest San Juan Mountains; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; San Juan River Basin; Southeast Utah; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.182c1c0ea2959b4257dcf57d2b97131c9d757412.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.182c1c0ea2959b4257dcf57d2b97131c9d757412.001.1"},{"event":"Hydrologic Outlook","headline":"Hydrologic Outlook issued October 29 at 10:53AM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"ESFGJT\n\nLow pressure begins to dig southward from the PACNW on Friday,\ntapping into a fetch of Pacific moisture that will evolve into an\natmospheric river streaming into the Desert Southwest this weekend.\nShowers and thunderstorms develop on Friday and Saturday across the\nregion. Lighter rainfall totals Friday and Saturday will reflect,\ninitially lower, moisture. However, a few thunderstorms could produce\nimpactful downpours on saturated soils left over from last weekend's\nhistoric rainfall.\n\nDeeper moisture develops Saturday night and early Sunday. This is\nexpected to produce heavier and more widespread rainfall across\nsoutheast UT and southwest CO Sunday and Monday, mostly below 10000\nfeet. While rivers are forecast to remain below action stage, a\ngrowing concern lies in the potential for runoff around saturated\nhillsides in the region. This could produce rock, mud, and debris\nflows onto roadways, as well as flash flood prone burn scars, washes,\nslot canyons, and arroyos. Early estimates predict an area of 1 to 2\ninches of new rainfall for portions of southeast UT and southwest CO.\n\nThis is a preliminary forecast that hinges on slight shifts in a\nstrong moisture plume impacting the region. It also attempts to\ncapture the most statistically likely outcome, despite stronger and\nweaker outlier predictions. Outdoor enthusiasts, motorists, and\nresidents of recent flood impacted areas are encouraged to remain\nabreast of the developing forecast in the coming days.","ends":"2026-10-30T15:00:00-06:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO; Dolores, CO; La Plata, CO; Montezuma, CO; San Miguel, CO; San Juan, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.8fb5d34f2d13ed8ac1ea289950d1e46b096a7bdd.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.8fb5d34f2d13ed8ac1ea289950d1e46b096a7bdd.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-10-30T19:32:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 301932 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Junction CO 132 PM MDT Fri Oct 30 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Showers, maybe a few storms, move across the area late Friday night into Saturday morning. Some light snow possible at the highest elevations. - Flooding/flash flooding concerns increase Sunday into Monday, especially for areas that have saturated soils fr","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"GJT","issued